meta {
  name: 5 Mark thread read
  type: http
  seq: 5
}
post {
  url: {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/messages/thread/{{customerEmail}}/read
  body: none
}
tests {
  test("200 with the number of messages marked", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
    expect(res.getBody()).to.be.a("number");
  });
}
docs {
  **The whole conversation in one write.** Opening a thread marks everything the counterpart sent read,
  and the client used to do that with one request — and one server-side read-then-replace — per message,
  so a thread with 250 unread messages was 250 sequential round trips behind a spinner.

  Keyed on the **counterpart's email**, exactly like `2 Get thread`: the server derives `thread_id` by
  sorting both addresses, so the caller always supplies one side and a thread between two other people
  has no representation in this URL space.

  Three path segments, so it cannot collide with the two-segment `{{messageId}}/read` route.

  **No ownership guard and no subscription check, deliberately.** The caller's own `sub` claim is one
  side of the derived thread id *and* the `recipient_email` in the update filter, so this can only ever
  clear rows addressed to the caller — there is no address in the request to substitute. Same reasoning
  as `8-Notifications/4 Mark all read`.

  `200 OK` with a bare integer: the count, so a client can word "nothing left to mark" differently from
  "could not reach the server" — those are indistinguishable if both arrive as `0`. An unknown
  counterpart is `200` with `0`, not `404`: answering differently would report whether a conversation
  exists.
}
